# Publishing the changes to Github

Whenever we want to publish the changes we've done in the Worktree.

## Comitting

Identify the files you've made changes, sometimes there will be modifications in some files that are related to each other, for this you have to commit those files under a single commit. 

**IMPORTANT** We use emojis to represent features, fixes and work in progress. For example: 

- ✅ feat: new modal for creating a new scammer...
- 🛠️ fix: creating new scammer modal wasn't working 
- 💻 wip: new authentication process

**IMPORTANT**, you have to add the model as Co-Author, appending `-m "Co-Authored-By: <Company> <Model> <email>"` to the `git commit ...` command.

Use the value that matches the model making the commit. Put the model version in the name. Keep that vendor's email.

Cursor (`cursoragent@cursor.com`):

- Grok 4.7: `Cursor Grok 4.7 <cursoragent@cursor.com>`
- Grok 4.6: `Cursor Grok 4.6 <cursoragent@cursor.com>`
- Grok 4.5: `Cursor Grok 4.5 <cursoragent@cursor.com>`
- Composer 2.5: `Cursor Composer 2.5 <cursoragent@cursor.com>`

Claude (`noreply@anthropic.com`):

- Opus 4.6: `Claude Opus 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>`
- Sonnet 4.6: `Claude Sonnet 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>`
- Haiku 4.5: `Claude Haiku 4.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>`

OpenAI (`noreply@openai.com`):

- GPT 5.4: `GPT 5.4 <noreply@openai.com>`
- Codex: `Codex <noreply@openai.com>`

Gemini (`218195315+gemini-cli@users.noreply.github.com`):

- Gemini 2.5 Pro: `Gemini 2.5 Pro <218195315+gemini-cli@users.noreply.github.com>`

GitHub Copilot (`copilot@github.com`):

- Copilot: `Copilot <copilot@github.com>`

Finally, after every change was committed, you have to push the Git Worktree `git push -u origin HEAD`

Examples:

- `git commit -m "✅ feat: new modal for creating a new scammer" -m "Co-Authored-By: Cursor Grok 4.7 <cursoragent@cursor.com>`
- `git commit -m "🛠️ fix: modal for creating a new scammer wasn't working" -m "Co-Authored-By: Cursor Grok 4.7 <cursoragent@cursor.com>`
- `git commit -m "💻 wip: new architecture improvement following Clean Architecture principles" -m "Co-Authored-By: Cursor Grok 4.7 <cursoragent@cursor.com>`

## Creating the Pull Request

We have to use `gh` command to create the pull request. **IMPORTANT** the environment variable `GITHUB_TOKEN` is already set, you don't need to manually sign in.

```bash
gh pr create --fill -B <target-branch> -H <head-branch>
```

Where `<target-branch>` is the branch where the changes will be merged and `<head-branch>` the branch we are currently.

The pull request body is only the `Co-Authored-By` line for the model that made the commit. Do not add a summary, test plan, or any other description. `--fill` is correct only when the commit body is already just that line. Otherwise set the body explicitly:

```bash
gh pr create -B <target-branch> -H <head-branch> \
  --title "<commit subject>" \
  --body "Co-Authored-By: Cursor Grok 4.7 <cursoragent@cursor.com>"
```

The response would be the following:

```bash
Creating pull request for <head-branch> into <target-branch> in Fraude-Bot/Frontend

https://github.com/Fraude-Bot/Frontend/pull/#
```

Return the pull request url.


